tokenizer_json.detail:
SentencePiece-derived BPE exported to the HF tokenizers format, 16384 entries. This is the ONLY substantive difference from the Apache sibling, which ships a GPT-2-style byte-level BPE. Normalizer: Sequence[Replace(
 -> 

), Precompiled(charsmap)]. pre_tokenizer: null. Decoder: Sequence[Replace(U+2581 -> ' '), ByteFallback, Fuse, Strip(' ', start=1)]. model.byte_fallback=true, unk_token=<unk>, 30161 merges (vs 16127 on the Apache sibling). Layout: id 0 = <unk> (also pad/CTC blank), ids 1..254 = 254 reserved placeholders <|tok0|>..<|tok253|>, ids 255..510 = byte fallback <0x00>..<0xFF>, ids 511..16383 = SentencePiece word pieces (12918 of the 16384 entries carry a leading U+2581).

known_risks:
TOKENIZER IS THE ONLY STRUCTURAL DELTA, and it is a different tokenizer FAMILY, not a different vocab. The Apache sibling is a GPT-2-style byte-level BPE (GGUF tokenizer.ggml.model="gpt2", DecodeMode::Gpt2ByteUnicode). This variant is a SentencePiece-derived BPE: normalizer Sequence[Replace(
 -> 

), Precompiled(charsmap)], pre_tokenizer null, decoder Sequence[Replace(U+2581 -> ' '), ByteFallback, Fuse, Strip(' ', start=1)], model.byte_fallback=true, 30161 merges. It must convert to GGUF tokenizer.ggml.model="bpe" so src/transcribe-tokenizer.cpp selects DecodeMode::SentencePiece. Emitting "gpt2" would silently produce mojibake on every U+2581, and tokenizer.ggml.pre="granite" is meaningless on the SentencePiece path and must not be carried over.
